- Story & Context: In the blistering July squall of 1994, fourteen-year-old Clara Vance climbed the iron mast of WTOR on Pine Mountain to play the county boys' dare game of counting down thunderclaps, clutching an ungrounded brass antenna lead in her calloused fist. When the bolt struck, the surge missed Clara's heart by inches, setting the vacuum tubes ablaze and broadcasting her fierce, ragged holler across three counties over the sound of shaking pine trees and rumbling oak skins. Neighbors three valleys away bolted from their rocking chairs as their kitchen radios began to howl with celestial organ tones and a blistering slide guitar that wasn't playing any hymn they knew. Clara’s mother watched the hillside ignite with purple St. Elmo’s fire just as the radio speaker spat out Clara's first recorded words: "We ain't running inside until the sky runs out of iron!"
