- Story & Context: Kenjiro Oka sat on his family's warped cabin porch during the August dog-day storms, grinding blue carpenter's chalk into sidewalk defense squares to play the solitary territorial game his late grandfather had taught him. Every time the thunder rattled the tin roof, Kenjiro struck his father's hand-carved nagadō-daiko with split hickory bachi, trying to drown out the sheriff's eviction notice stapled to the porch beam. The scent of rain-soaked slate, ozone, and wet cedar filled his lungs as Clara’s pirate station signal abruptly broke through the porch-side transistor, matching his erratic taiko downbeats with an agonizing, orbital slide riff that pulled the breath clean out of his chest. Bleeding through his knuckles onto the drum skin, Kenjiro shouted his confession straight into the teeth of the cloudburst: "I beat this wet wood till my shame turns into orbit."
